#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Weekly, redacted digest of every IBP app's warnings and errors.

Runs as root on ibp-server (ibp-error-digest.timer, Thursdays 09:00) and:

1. reads the events of the week that ended at the latest scheduled run
   (Thursday 09:00 America/Chicago, since <= ts < until, so a late run or a
   DST change leaves no gap between digests) from
   - /srv/ibp-logs/<station>/errors-*.jsonl (the shared JSONL format), where
     a Windows laptop uploads,
   - /srv/ibp-logs/<station>/<host>/, where a kiosk uploads: errors-*.jsonl,
     print-server/errors-*.jsonl, and journal/*.jsonl (its journal export,
     in journald's field names, an entry exported twice counted once by its
     __CURSOR, and failed-units-<day>.jsonl),
   - /srv/ibp/logs/*.jsonl (backend.jsonl, client-errors.jsonl), and
   - the journals of the ibp-* units at warning or above, and the failed
     units (journalctl, systemctl);
2. groups them by fingerprint: app, exception type and the innermost frame
   in IBP code; for the browser apps, logger, HTTP status, exception type and
   message shape; or for an event with no exception its message with
   numbers, codes and quoted text replaced by placeholders;
3. compares the groups with STATE/seen.json (counts and dates only) to mark
   each new, recurring, growing (up by half or more on last week) or gone;
   groups whose fingerprint is in CONFIG/muted are counted but left out of
   the report;
4. checks the stations: a folder with no file uploaded all week, and each
   station's app versions from its "starting" lines, next to the deploy
   branches' and the release's versions;
5. writes DIGEST/<YYYY>-W<ww>.md and .json (root, group adm, 0640; 26 weeks
   kept), and
6. pings Healthchecks if CONFIG/ping-url exists; without it, no ping.

If a source can't be read, the digest is still written, its first line says
it is incomplete, seen.json is left as it was, and the exit status is 1.

Redaction: no ``data`` field (but a browser app's HTTP status), request body
or raw line is ever copied. Each message, and the exception line of a stack,
has secrets (a URL's password, Bearer/Basic credentials, key=, token=,
password=... values, JWTs, long base64 or hex runs) and e-mail addresses
replaced. Inmate names, TDCJ numbers, addresses and package codes are kept
(largely public, and useful); fingerprints still ignore them. A stack is kept as
frames only (file:line function; a bundle's minified names left out). The
raw events stay where they are; each group names one of them (path:line,
only for a path of plain names, or a journalctl command) to look up on the
Pi with ``--show-raw PATH:LINE``, which prints that one event's fields but
``data``, opening each path component without following a symlink.

Exit status: 0 the digest was written (whatever it found), 1 it could not
be written or a source could not be read (the digest is still written if it
can be), 2 bad arguments, 75 another run holds the lock. --show-raw: 0
printed, 1 no such regular file or line, 2 a path it doesn't take.

Python 3 standard library only.
"""

